WEBVTT

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:07.978
Welcome to CSC4801, AI-assisted Software Engineering.

00:00:07.978 --> 00:00:10.660
This is Lecture 9: Coding 2.

00:00:11.433 --> 00:00:17.273
We are still in the coding stage of the software life cycle, between requirements and testing.

00:00:17.623 --> 00:00:25.144
Remember the 3 pillars of AI for coding: the model, the harness around it, and the benchmarks that measure it.

00:00:25.144 --> 00:00:29.383
Today we look at 2 of them. First the harness, then the model.

00:00:29.733 --> 00:00:31.759
We start with a hard question.

00:00:31.759 --> 00:00:37.973
How do we handle code at the level of a whole repository, where the context is far too long?

00:00:38.523 --> 00:00:40.407
There are 3 challenges.

00:00:40.407 --> 00:00:45.403
First, the context is simply too long to fit into one prompt.

00:00:45.753 --> 00:00:48.036
Second, files depend on each other.

00:00:48.036 --> 00:00:54.753
The line you are writing may call a shared utility, or read a configuration, that lives somewhere else.

00:00:55.103 --> 00:01:02.283
Third, every repository has its own structure: its naming conventions and its coding style.

00:01:02.283 --> 00:01:06.543
Code that ignores them may run, but it doesn't belong.

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:11.347
One early answer is called Repo-Level Prompt Generation.

00:01:11.347 --> 00:01:20.093
Its idea is to build the prompt from the repository itself, mixing in its structure and relevant context from other files.

00:01:20.443 --> 00:01:22.931
Here is the example from the paper.

00:01:22.931 --> 00:01:30.963
We are editing a Java file, and the cursor sits at a hole: the model must complete a call on an object called mg.

00:01:31.313 --> 00:01:34.029
The method we need is not in this file.

00:01:34.029 --> 00:01:38.833
It is defined in another file, the one that the current file imports.

00:01:39.183 --> 00:01:49.023
A prompt engineer prepares a list of prompt proposals, each one a different place to take context from: the imported file, a sibling file, and so on.

00:01:49.373 --> 00:01:54.259
A classifier looks at the hole and predicts which proposal will help most.

00:01:54.259 --> 00:01:57.693
Here it picks proposal number 14, the imported file.

00:01:58.043 --> 00:02:04.251
A prompt composer then puts that context in front of the usual code before the hole.

00:02:04.251 --> 00:02:10.163
Now the model, Codex, can see the method it needs, and fills the hole correctly.

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:18.483
This is retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG: first retrieve what is relevant, then generate.

00:02:19.266 --> 00:02:23.261
A method called Repo Coder takes this one step further.

00:02:23.261 --> 00:02:31.106
It combines a similarity-based retriever with a pre-trained code language model, and it runs them in a loop.

00:02:31.456 --> 00:02:33.951
Look at this unfinished function.

00:02:33.951 --> 00:02:40.376
It copies images, downscales them, and then has to call Col Map, an external program.

00:02:40.726 --> 00:02:47.264
In the first iteration, the retriever searches the repository for code similar to the unfinished code.

00:02:47.264 --> 00:02:51.046
It finds a function that also copies and downscales images.

00:02:51.396 --> 00:02:53.654
The generator writes a completion.

00:02:53.654 --> 00:02:55.048
But it guesses wrong.

00:02:55.048 --> 00:03:00.956
It calls a function it names Col Map, with arguments that don't exist in this repository.

00:03:01.306 --> 00:03:02.714
Pause and think.

00:03:02.714 --> 00:03:07.466
Is this wrong answer useless? Or can it still help us?

00:03:08.016 --> 00:03:09.369
Repo Coder uses it.

00:03:09.369 --> 00:03:18.056
The failed draft names Col Map and its path arguments, so it is a much better search query than the unfinished code alone.

00:03:18.406 --> 00:03:28.566
In the second iteration, the retriever searches again with the draft, and now it finds the real helper, a function named run_col_map, with its true parameters.

00:03:28.916 --> 00:03:32.083
The second completion uses it correctly.

00:03:32.083 --> 00:03:37.072
Retrieve, generate, and retrieve again with what you generated.

00:03:37.072 --> 00:03:40.476
That is iterative retrieval and generation.

00:03:41.266 --> 00:03:44.970
Even with good retrieval, there's another danger.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:50.034
LLM-generated code may use packages or functions that do not exist.

00:03:50.034 --> 00:03:52.226
This is called hallucination.

00:03:52.576 --> 00:03:53.784
And it is common.

00:03:53.784 --> 00:04:03.015
A study at the USENIX Security conference measured how often many code models invent package names, in both Python and JavaScript.

00:04:03.015 --> 00:04:05.216
Every model they tested did it.

00:04:05.566 --> 00:04:06.949
Why does it matter?

00:04:06.949 --> 00:04:15.246
Your build fails, or worse: someone can publish a real package under the invented name, and your code installs it.

00:04:15.596 --> 00:04:17.507
The fix can be simple.

00:04:17.507 --> 00:04:23.932
Suppose the model writes import pandes, spelled with an E instead of an A.

00:04:23.932 --> 00:04:29.836
Before trusting it, check the name against the Python Package Index.

00:04:30.186 --> 00:04:34.868
There is no package spelled that way, but there is pandas.

00:04:34.868 --> 00:04:39.146
So we correct the import, and only then run the code.

00:04:39.933 --> 00:04:44.933
Today, one approach dominates all of these techniques: autonomous agents.

00:04:45.283 --> 00:04:52.986
Instead of a fixed retriever, an agent decides for itself what to search, which files to read, and when to run the code.

00:04:52.986 --> 00:04:56.323
Retrieval and checking become tools inside its loop.

00:04:56.673 --> 00:04:59.065
We can even use several agents.

00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:03.230
A framework called Meta GPT copies a software company.

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:11.793
It gives each agent a role: a product manager, an architect, a project manager, an engineer, and a QA engineer.

00:05:12.143 --> 00:05:15.151
They follow a standard operating procedure.

00:05:15.151 --> 00:05:26.063
A one-line request, such as write a simple Flappy Bird game, becomes a requirement document, then a system design, then tasks, then code, and finally tests.

00:05:26.413 --> 00:05:31.013
Each agent checks the work of the one before it, just like a real team.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:35.193
Now let's turn to the second pillar: the model.

00:05:35.193 --> 00:05:38.080
And a model is only as good as its data.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:43.284
The training datasets of notable AI models have grown enormously over the years.

00:05:43.284 --> 00:05:45.710
We need more and more high-quality data.

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:50.935
Human-written data, like a carefully built benchmark, is high quality.

00:05:50.935 --> 00:05:55.740
But it is labor-intensive to make, and it may raise privacy concerns.

00:05:56.090 --> 00:06:01.970
So people use synthetic data: data generated by models themselves.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:05.581
The simplest recipe is called Self-Instruct.

00:06:05.581 --> 00:06:07.839
Give a model a few seed problems.

00:06:07.839 --> 00:06:10.850
It writes new problems, and their solutions.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:16.680
Those problem and solution pairs can train the model itself, or train other models.

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:20.589
A famous example is Alpaca, from Stanford.

00:06:20.589 --> 00:06:24.910
They started with 175 seed tasks written by people.

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:31.301
A strong OpenAI model expanded them into 52,000 instruction-following examples.

00:06:31.301 --> 00:06:36.500
Then they fine-tuned Meta's 7-billion-parameter LLaMA model on them.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:42.885
Training a small model on a big model's outputs has a name: knowledge distillation.

00:06:42.885 --> 00:06:47.460
A large teacher model passes its knowledge to a small student model.

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:49.160
Here's an example.

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:50.585
Is this email spam?

00:06:50.585 --> 00:06:53.210
You won a free phone, claim it now.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:07:01.000
The teacher answers with scores: 90% spam, 8% promotion, 2% normal.

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:04.103
A hard label would only say spam.

00:07:04.103 --> 00:07:11.861
But the teacher's soft scores carry more: promotion is a much closer alternative than normal.

00:07:11.861 --> 00:07:15.030
The student learns that whole pattern.

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:17.592
The recipe has 4 steps.

00:07:17.592 --> 00:07:21.687
Train the teacher on correctly labeled examples.

00:07:21.687 --> 00:07:25.270
Ask the teacher for scores on many inputs.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:28.742
Soften those scores with a higher temperature.

00:07:28.742 --> 00:07:34.036
Then train the student to match both the teacher's scores and the true labels.

00:07:34.036 --> 00:07:36.140
The loss is the sum of the two.

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:46.250
The student can learn different things: the teacher's outputs, its intermediate layers, or the relationships between layers or between data samples.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:47.480
Why distill?

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:51.514
Serving a large model can be too slow or too expensive.

00:07:51.514 --> 00:07:55.400
The student is smaller, faster, and easier to deploy.

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:56.918
But watch out.

00:07:56.918 --> 00:08:04.590
The student may lose some capability, and it can copy the teacher's mistakes and biases too.

00:08:05.367 --> 00:08:07.965
Self-Instruct makes more problems.

00:08:07.965 --> 00:08:10.181
But we also want harder ones.

00:08:10.181 --> 00:08:18.127
A method called Evol-Instruct takes a seed problem and an evolution type, and writes an evolved problem.

00:08:18.477 --> 00:08:20.270
Start from 1 plus 1.

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:27.083
Add a constraint. Go deeper. Make it concrete. Ask for more reasoning steps.

00:08:27.083 --> 00:08:33.357
Step by step, a simple instruction turns into a much more complex one.

00:08:33.707 --> 00:08:38.207
Another method, OSS-Instruct, starts from real code instead.

00:08:38.207 --> 00:08:42.707
It takes a snippet from an open-source repository as a seed.

00:08:43.057 --> 00:08:50.417
The model is asked to take inspiration from the snippet, and write a new, high-quality programming problem with its solution.

00:08:50.767 --> 00:09:00.647
Because the seeds come from many real projects, the problems are more varied and less biased than ones a model invents from nothing.

00:09:01.247 --> 00:09:10.167
In the Magicoder paper, models trained this way compare well with other models of the same size on HumanEval, a standard coding benchmark.

00:09:10.967 --> 00:09:14.558
With data in hand, how do we build a coding LLM?

00:09:14.558 --> 00:09:17.925
Training one from scratch is super expensive.

00:09:17.925 --> 00:09:23.087
The practical way is to start from a base model and keep training it.

00:09:23.437 --> 00:09:29.282
Composer 2, the model inside the Cursor editor, starts from the base model Kimi 2.5.

00:09:29.282 --> 00:09:36.517
It continues pretraining on more data, then uses reinforcement learning inside Cursor's own environment.

00:09:36.867 --> 00:09:41.709
The second model, Coder Next from Alibaba, follows the same shape.

00:09:41.709 --> 00:09:44.717
Its base is Alibaba's general Next model.

00:09:44.717 --> 00:09:53.667
Then comes mid-training, and then reinforcement learning, with tasks built from GitHub pull requests in Docker containers.

00:09:54.017 --> 00:09:57.766
To get enough of those tasks, they synthesize them.

00:09:57.766 --> 00:10:04.235
They collect repositories, plant bugs, and keep only the bugs that make real tests fail.

00:10:04.235 --> 00:10:08.057
Then a model writes an issue that describes the bug.

00:10:08.657 --> 00:10:14.337
Each one becomes a new task: here is a repository and an issue, now fix it.

00:10:15.133 --> 00:10:18.775
Both models end with reinforcement learning.

00:10:18.775 --> 00:10:24.486
There are many algorithms for LLMs, such as PPO, DPO, GRPO, and GSPO,

00:10:24.486 --> 00:10:28.293
and mature open-source frameworks to run them.

00:10:28.643 --> 00:10:33.567
But for software engineering tasks, the critical part is the reward.

00:10:33.567 --> 00:10:35.883
How do we score a piece of code?

00:10:36.483 --> 00:10:38.681
There are two kinds of rewards.

00:10:38.681 --> 00:10:41.943
Implicit rewards use another model as a judge.

00:10:41.943 --> 00:10:45.843
Verifiable rewards come from actually running the code.

00:10:46.193 --> 00:10:48.805
Here is a reward model at work.

00:10:48.805 --> 00:10:53.187
The task: implement a discount calculation function.

00:10:53.187 --> 00:10:56.894
One answer just multiplies the price by 0.8.

00:10:56.894 --> 00:10:58.073
It scores 0.2.

00:10:58.423 --> 00:11:03.199
The other answer first rejects a negative price, then applies the discount.

00:11:03.199 --> 00:11:04.663
The judge gives it 0.8.

00:11:05.013 --> 00:11:07.409
Verifiable rewards are stricter.

00:11:07.409 --> 00:11:10.105
If all tests pass, the reward is +1.

00:11:10.105 --> 00:11:17.293
If any test fails, the code doesn't compile, or it runs out of time or memory, the reward is -1.

00:11:18.066 --> 00:11:21.506
But this creates a problem called sparse reward.

00:11:21.856 --> 00:11:29.573
Imagine the model writes a 500-line program, with one off-by-one error on line 499.

00:11:29.573 --> 00:11:30.596
It gets -1.

00:11:30.596 --> 00:11:35.616
No partial credit, and no hint of where it went wrong.

00:11:36.216 --> 00:11:38.672
One fix is fine-grained feedback.

00:11:38.672 --> 00:11:43.136
Instead of one reward at the end, give rewards step by step.

00:11:43.486 --> 00:11:47.809
In this function, line 6 has a typo: 'dta' instead of 'data',

00:11:47.809 --> 00:11:49.651
so it throws a name error.

00:11:49.651 --> 00:11:53.406
Only the tokens that led to that error are penalized.

00:11:53.756 --> 00:11:57.253
The valid early return is not penalized.

00:11:57.253 --> 00:12:02.236
And line 7 never ran, so it is masked out of the penalty.

00:12:02.586 --> 00:12:07.424
Static analysis tools, like a linter, can also give rewards.

00:12:07.424 --> 00:12:09.923
A syntax error costs 10 points.

00:12:09.923 --> 00:12:11.858
A SQL injection costs 5.

00:12:11.858 --> 00:12:14.035
An unused variable costs 1.

00:12:14.035 --> 00:12:18.066
A missing docstring costs only a fifth of a point.

00:12:18.416 --> 00:12:21.982
Finally, we can reward the reasoning itself.

00:12:21.982 --> 00:12:27.816
A process reward model scores the chain of thought: 0.8, highly logical.

00:12:28.166 --> 00:12:32.870
But the final code fails to compile, so its outcome is 0.

00:12:32.870 --> 00:12:36.253
Multiply them, and the total reward is 0.

00:12:36.253 --> 00:12:39.966
Good reasoning only counts if the code works.

00:12:40.766 --> 00:12:44.130
So, should we use a general model or a coding model?

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:51.246
Leaderboards rank models on software engineering and on code generation, and the ranking changes all the time.

00:12:51.596 --> 00:12:53.912
The bigger question is this.

00:12:53.912 --> 00:12:57.716
Do LLMs really make developers more efficient?

00:12:58.266 --> 00:13:03.427
A McKinsey study says yes, but less so for hard tasks.

00:13:03.427 --> 00:13:10.212
With generative AI, code documentation took about 45% to 50% less time,

00:13:10.212 --> 00:13:14.226
and code generation about 35% to 45% less.

00:13:14.576 --> 00:13:16.883
Refactoring saved 20% to 30%.

00:13:16.883 --> 00:13:21.336
But for high-complexity tasks, the saving was under 10%.

00:13:21.686 --> 00:13:23.597
And speed is not quality.

00:13:23.597 --> 00:13:31.010
Another study, from Uplevel, found that developers with Copilot access had a 41% higher bug rate,

00:13:31.010 --> 00:13:35.366
while the number of issues they finished stayed the same.

00:13:35.966 --> 00:13:40.846
So engineering teams still need to review the code, and put guardrails in place.

00:13:41.633 --> 00:13:42.953
Let's sum up.

00:13:43.303 --> 00:13:46.834
Repository-level code is too long for one prompt.

00:13:46.834 --> 00:13:52.022
So we retrieve the right context, and RepoCoder does it again and again,

00:13:52.022 --> 00:13:54.543
using each draft as a better query.

00:13:54.893 --> 00:14:00.602
Generated code can invent packages that don't exist, so check names before you run it.

00:14:00.602 --> 00:14:04.253
And today, agents fold all these steps into their loop.

00:14:04.603 --> 00:14:11.442
Coding models are built from a base model with synthetic data, continued training, and reinforcement learning,

00:14:11.442 --> 00:14:14.363
where the reward decides what the model learns.

00:14:14.713 --> 00:14:16.286
Here's something to try.

00:14:16.286 --> 00:14:21.658
Ask a coding assistant to finish a function that calls a helper from another file.

00:14:21.658 --> 00:14:23.755
Does it guess the helper's name?

00:14:23.755 --> 00:14:26.113
Then show it that file, and compare.

00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:28.489
That's all for this lecture.

00:14:28.489 --> 00:14:29.793
Thank you for watching.

00:14:29.793 --> 00:14:33.140
Bring your questions to the next class, Team Collaboration.

